Serialize SQL reads on the shared connection

A @read_api method holds the shared read lock, so many threads can run them
at once. Most read only the in-memory tables, but some run SQL on the
backend's single apsw connection, which allows one call at a time. A second
thread then fails with

    apsw.ThreadingViolationError: Cursor couldn't run because the
    Connection is busy in another thread

The content server shows this as HTTP 500s from 4 concurrent clients up,
mostly from /interface-data/get-books and /interface-data/book-metadata,
which read notes for every book (srv/metadata.py -> items_with_notes_in_book).

This adds a connection lock to DB and takes it, always inside the read lock,
in every read_api method that runs SQL (the new sql_read_api decorator), in
the page-count thread's get_batch() and in the FTS dispatcher's
get_next_fts_job(). Nothing inside the connection lock takes another lock or
waits on a thread, so it cannot form a cycle with the read/write lock.
Writers hold the exclusive lock and never run beside a reader, so they need
no change.

The methods were found by auditing every read_api method in db/cache.py
that touches self.backend. Those left alone read only an attribute, the
prefs dict or the file system: last_modified, is_fts_enabled,
field_supports_notes, pref, size_stats, format_hash, the cover_* and
copy_*_to methods, format_abspath, has_format, formats, read_backup,
get_top_level_move_items, the trash readers, are_paths_inside_book_dir and
list_extra_files.

calibre's db tests pass (99 run, 0 failures). A forced-interleaving test
that reproduces the error (calibre-zen's src/calibre_zen/tests/test_srvfix.py)
passes with this change.

